<PracticeAreaLayout
  title="Family Matters Legal Help | Maine Volunteer Lawyers Project"
  description="VLP provides free legal help for family law matters including divorce, parental rights, child custody and support, and court order modifications."
  heading="Family Matters"
  intro="Free legal assistance for divorce, parental rights and responsibilities, child custody and support, and other family court proceedings."
  ctaHeading="Ready to get help with your family law case?"
  ctaText="Complete an online intake form to request a clinic appointment. VLP staff will review your submission and follow up with you."
>

  <div slot="body">

    <h2>About Family Matters</h2>
    <p>
      "Family matters" is a term used to describe cases dealt with in family
      court, including divorce, parental rights and responsibilities, a motion
      to modify or enforce a current court order, and/or a motion for contempt.
    </p>

    <h3>Divorce</h3>
    <p>
      The legal process to end a marriage. The Maine Judicial Branch has
      <a href="https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/family/divorce-separation/divorce.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">guides and forms for divorce</a>,
      including a
      <a href="https://www.courts.maine.gov/help/guides/family-separation-guide.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Family Separation Guide (PDF)</a>
      and an overview of the
      <a href="https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/family/process.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">family court process</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Parental Rights and Responsibilities</h3>
    <p>
      For unmarried parents establishing individual parental rights and
      responsibilities. Maine law presumes equal parental rights when no
      court order exists. Court decisions address where children live,
      parent-child contact plans, decision-making authority, and child
      support. The court aims for equal parenting time and joint
      decision-making where possible, using
      <a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/19-A/title19-Asec1653.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">"best interest of the child" factors</a>.
      See also the Maine Courts page on
      <a href="https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/family/divorce-separation/decisions-children.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">decisions about children</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Child Support</h3>
    <p>
      Child support is about the right of the child to be financially
      supported by their parents, not a parent's right to receive child
      support from the other parent. When parents do not live together,
      often one parent will pay money to the other as child support,
      whether they are married or not married. The state provides a
      <a href="https://gateway.maine.gov/DHHS/cswswebnew/Applications/csws/index.aspx" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">child support worksheet and calculator</a>
      and
      <a href="https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/ofi/programs-services/child-support-services" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">child support services through DHHS</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Changing or Enforcing a Court Order</h3>
    <p>
      A divorce or parental rights and responsibilities order may be changed
      by filing a motion to modify. A motion to modify is generally limited
      to child-related matters, child support, or spousal support. If the
      other party is not following a court order, you can file a motion to
      enforce. Out-of-state orders must be registered with Maine courts
      before they can be modified or enforced here. The Maine Judicial Branch
      has information on
      <a href="https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/family/changing.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">changing or enforcing family court orders</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Motion for Contempt</h3>
    <p>
      A more serious enforcement alternative used when the other party is
      not following a court order. Consequences of being found in contempt
      can include a fine or jail time.
    </p>

    <h2>About This Clinic</h2>

    <div class="notice-box">
      <p>
        Services are free with income-based eligibility. To request an
        appointment, complete the online intake form. A VLP staff member will
        follow up within one to two weeks. If you have an upcoming court date
        or deadline, please note it in your submission.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Courthouse Assistance Project (CHAP)</h3>
    <p>
      CHAP provides 30-minute consultation appointments via Zoom (with video
      and phone options available). In-person meetings are also available at
      Lewiston District Court two Wednesdays per month on a first-come,
      first-served basis.
    </p>
    <p>
      Volunteer attorneys can provide legal advice, answer questions about
      your case, explain court processes, and help you prepare for an
      upcoming court event. Our volunteers cannot represent you in court.
      You can have as many CHAP appointments as you need during your case,
      subject to appointment availability.
    </p>
    <p>
      To apply, complete the intake form online. A VLP staff member will
      contact you once we have reviewed your submission.
    </p>

    <h3>Helpline</h3>
    <p>
      The Helpline is specifically for family law cases involving domestic
      violence. This clinic provides phone consultations on Wednesdays from
      5:30 pm to 8:00 pm with a volunteer attorney who can provide legal
      advice, answer questions, and help you prepare for an upcoming court
      event. Our volunteers cannot represent you in court.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cases for the Helpline are by referral only  -  referrals come from
      domestic violence resource centers or by VLP staff designation.
    </p>

